#---
# purpose: "a non-tail recursion whose return type is a nesting ladder: {n, {n, ... :start}}"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# The program itself is ordinary and correct on every door. What it pins is the
# compiler's cost of inferring its return. `build/1` returns `:start` or a pair
# whose second field is another `build/1` return, so each round of the fixpoint
# learns one more level of nesting and the inferred return climbs a ladder
# instead of reaching a recursive denotation. Past the widening budget the
# ladder tops out at `any`. `compiler2_tuple_return_ladder_revises_once_per_nesting_level`
# in the drive tests measures that cost.
def build(0), do: :start
def build(n), do: {n, build(n - 1)}

def main() do
  dbg(build(3))
end
